    "documentation": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "content": "**Scientific study:** [dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures on GitHub](https://github.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures)  \n**Dataset source:** [R `datasets::nottem` documentation](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/nottem.html)\n\nThis dataset presents a **univariate time-series forecasting** problem based on monthly average air temperatures recorded at Nottingham Castle from 1920 through 1939.\n\nThe objective is to characterize the temporal structure of the series, compare fixed forecasting specifications against meaningful baselines under temporally ordered backtesting, select one model without future leakage, and evaluate the frozen specification on the final 1939 holdout.\n\nThe analysis is predictive rather than causal. Trend, seasonality, autocorrelation, and forecast performance describe statistical structure in the observed record; they do not identify causal climate mechanisms.\n\n## Dataset overview\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Monthly observations | 240 |\n| Coverage | `1920-01` → `1939-12` |\n| Target | `temperature` |\n| Target unit | degrees Fahrenheit (°F) |\n| Frequency | monthly |\n| Source exogenous predictors | none |\n| Forecast horizon | 12 months |\n| Problem type | Univariate forecasting |\n| Development history | 228 observations |\n| Final holdout | 12 observations |\n| Selected model | Deterministic Seasonal-Trend OLS |\n| Primary metric | MAE |\n| Backtesting MAE | **1.839438 °F** |\n| Final-holdout MAE | **1.526584 °F** |\n\nThe canonical forecasting series contains a monthly time index and one endogenous numerical target.\n\nThe source transport representation is reconstructed into a monthly period index before analysis. Time is not treated as an ordinary tabular feature, and no external regressors or exogenous predictors are available.\n\n## Forecasting question\n\nThe analysis investigates three related questions:\n\n1. **What temporal structure is present in Nottingham monthly temperatures?**\n2. **Can a learned forecasting specification outperform a 12-month seasonal-naive reference under expanding-window backtesting?**\n3. **How does the frozen selected specification perform on the final 1939 holdout?**\n\nForecasting requires preservation of temporal order. Random train/test shuffling or ordinary K-fold evaluation would expose models to future context and is therefore inappropriate.\n\n## Data quality and temporal preparation\n\nThe canonical series contains 240 consecutive monthly observations.\n\nThe study finds:\n\n- no missing canonical months;\n- no duplicated canonical periods;\n- no missing target values;\n- no non-finite temperatures;\n- no source exogenous predictors;\n- no point anomalies meeting the predefined robust intervention threshold;\n- no need for row deletion, interpolation, clipping, winsorization, or synthetic filling.\n\nThe series remains on its original Fahrenheit scale.\n\nNo global logarithmic transformation, Box-Cox transform, learned scaling, full-series detrending, globally fitted decomposition, or mandatory preprocessing differencing is applied.\n\n### Temporal boundary\n\n| Scope | Range | Observations | Purpose |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| Development | `1920-01` → `1938-12` | 228 | Backtesting and final model fitting |\n| Final holdout | `1939-01` → `1939-12` | 12 | One-time final evaluation |\n\nThe final forecast origin is `1938-12`.\n\nFrom the preparation stage onward, the 1939 holdout is excluded from model fitting, candidate comparison, metric-driven selection, retuning, and specification adjustment until its single final evaluation.\n\n### Exploration-boundary caveat\n\nThe initial exploratory notebook retrospectively inspects the complete 1920–1939 source.\n\nThe final 1939 window is therefore prospectively sealed for **model selection and final evaluation**, but it is not an exploration-blind external test set in the strongest sense.\n\n## Historical temperature evolution\n\nThe 240 observations have approximately:\n\n| Statistic | Temperature |\n|---|---:|\n| Mean | 49.04 °F |\n| Median | 47.35 °F |\n| Minimum | 31.3 °F |\n| Maximum | 66.5 °F |\n\n![Monthly Nottingham temperature evolution](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures/main/docs/images/nottem_temperature_evolution.png)\n\nThe later years are modestly warmer on average than the earliest years, but the long-run movement is much smaller than the recurring within-year seasonal variation.\n\nThe descriptive annual-mean slope is approximately `0.057 °F/year`, and the mean during `1935–1939` is approximately `0.76 °F` above the mean during `1920–1924`.\n\nThese values motivate consideration of trend terms but do not establish a causal climate trend.\n\n## Seasonal structure\n\nAnnual seasonality is the dominant systematic pattern.\n\nJuly has the highest cross-year calendar-month mean and February the lowest. The calendar-month mean range is approximately **22.71 °F**.\n\n![Calendar-month Nottingham temperature profile](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures/main/docs/images/nottem_calendar_month_profile.png)\n\nSTL diagnostics estimate seasonal strength at approximately `0.960`, compared with trend strength of approximately `0.241`.\n\n![STL decomposition of Nottingham monthly temperatures](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures/main/docs/images/nottem_stl_decomposition.png)\n\nThis strong annual pattern motivates use of a 12-month seasonal-naive forecast as the primary baseline.\n\n## Target distribution and temporal dependence\n\n![Distribution of Nottingham monthly average temperatures](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures/main/docs/images/nottem_target_distribution.png)\n\nThe marginal distribution combines observations from different seasons and should not be interpreted independently from their calendar positions.\n\nThe strongest absolute autocorrelation within 36 months occurs at lag 12, at approximately `0.884` on the complete series and `0.886` within development history.\n\n![Autocorrelation diagnostics for Nottingham monthly temperatures](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures/main/docs/images/nottem_autocorrelation.png)\n\nThese diagnostics support explicit temporal modeling, but they are not converted into globally learned future-aware preprocessing features.\n\n## Evaluation protocol\n\nModel development uses **expanding-window backtesting**.\n\n| Property | Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Initial training history | 120 months |\n| Forecast horizon per fold | 12 months |\n| Origin step | 12 months |\n| Backtesting folds | 9 |\n| Validation years | 1930 → 1938 |\n| Forecasts per complete specification | 108 |\n\nAt each origin, a specification is fitted only with the history available at that time and must produce all 12 forecasts without consulting the next year's target values.\n\nValidation windows do not overlap.\n\n### Metrics\n\n| Metric | Interpretation |\n|---|---|\n| **MAE** | Primary metric; average absolute forecast error in °F |\n| RMSE | Secondary metric; gives greater weight to larger misses |\n| Seasonal MASE(12) | Secondary scale-free metric relative to seasonal-naive error scale |\n| Horizon-wise MAE | Diagnostic evidence across forecast steps |\n\nFor all three aggregate error metrics, **lower values are better**.\n\n`MAPE` and `sMAPE` are excluded because Fahrenheit has an arbitrary zero point, so percentage-of-observed-value error is not physically stable for this target.\n\n## Forecasting baselines\n\n| Baseline | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| `seasonal_naive_12` | Forecast each month from the same month one year earlier |\n| `naive_last_value` | Forecast every horizon using the last observed value |\n\nThe seasonal-naive specification is the primary benchmark because strong 12-month seasonality is already visible in the series.\n\n## Model selection\n\nThe frozen experiment contains **two baselines and eight learned forecasting specifications**.\n\nThe candidates are not generated by a hyperparameter-search algorithm. Each is a predefined fixed specification that is reconstructed and fitted from scratch within every authorized training fold.\n\n### Complete candidate and baseline comparison\n\n| Specification | Family | Status | MAE ↓ | RMSE ↓ | Seasonal MASE(12) ↓ | Improvement vs seasonal naive |\n|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| **`seasonal_trend_ols`** | DeterministicSeasonalTrendOLS | **Selected** | **1.839438** | 2.320801 | **0.656960** | **30.27%** |\n| `holt_winters_additive_no_trend` | ExponentialSmoothing | Practical-tie finalist | 1.850122 | 2.338465 | 0.660751 | 29.87% |\n| `sarima_100_011_12` | SARIMAX | Practical-tie finalist | 1.852289 | **2.305228** | 0.661688 | 29.78% |\n| `holt_winters_additive_damped_trend` | ExponentialSmoothing | Practical-tie finalist | 1.853886 | 2.342469 | 0.662140 | 29.72% |\n| `holt_winters_additive_trend` | ExponentialSmoothing | Practical-tie finalist | 1.857710 | 2.343873 | 0.663509 | 29.58% |\n| `autoreg_lag_1_2_12_ct` | AutoReg | Eligible | 2.376971 | 2.993368 | 0.848966 | 9.89% |\n| `autoreg_lag_1_12_ct` | AutoReg | Eligible | 2.607713 | 3.292361 | 0.931090 | 1.15% |\n| `seasonal_naive_12` | SeasonalNaive | Primary baseline | 2.637963 | 3.338898 | 0.941686 | — |\n| `naive_last_value` | NaiveLastValue | Secondary baseline | 10.563889 | 13.267843 | 3.770738 | -300.46% |\n| `sarima_100_100_12` | SARIMAX | **Incomplete / ineligible** | — | — | — | — |\n\nThe incomplete SARIMA specification completed only 2 of 9 folds before its explicit optimizer-failure policy blocked it. Its absence of pooled metrics is therefore intentional and documented rather than treated as missing evidence.\n\n![Backtesting MAE comparison across eligible forecasting specifications](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures/main/docs/images/nottem_model_selection_mae.png)\n\n### Candidate configurations and hyperparameters\n\n| Specification | Fixed configuration |\n|---|---|\n| `seasonal_naive_12` | Seasonal lag `12`; known-history lookup |\n| `naive_last_value` | Constant final observed value |\n| `seasonal_trend_ols` | Intercept; linear time trend; 11 month dummies; January reference |\n| `holt_winters_additive_no_trend` | `trend=None`; additive seasonality; `seasonal_periods=12`; estimated initialization; no Box-Cox |\n| `holt_winters_additive_damped_trend` | Additive damped trend; additive seasonality; `seasonal_periods=12`; estimated initialization; no Box-Cox |\n| `holt_winters_additive_trend` | Additive undamped trend; additive seasonality; `seasonal_periods=12`; estimated initialization; no Box-Cox |\n| `autoreg_lag_1_12_ct` | `lags=[1,12]`; constant + linear trend; no built-in seasonal term |\n| `autoreg_lag_1_2_12_ct` | `lags=[1,2,12]`; constant + linear trend; no built-in seasonal term |\n| `sarima_100_100_12` | `order=(1,0,0)`; `seasonal_order=(1,0,0,12)`; constant + linear trend; stationarity/invertibility enforced |\n| `sarima_100_011_12` | `order=(1,0,0)`; `seasonal_order=(0,1,1,12)`; no trend; fold-local seasonal differencing; stationarity/invertibility enforced |\n\nThis fixed-specification design is methodologically different from an AutoML or parameter-search workflow: the comparison is among explicitly declared forecasting hypotheses.\n\n### Practical-tie rule\n\nThe best raw pooled MAE is approximately:\n\n```text\n1.839438 °F\n```\n\nFive candidates fall within the frozen `0.05 °F` practical-tie tolerance.\n\nThe deterministic tie-break order is:\n\n1. Seasonal MASE(12);\n2. RMSE;\n3. fold-to-fold MAE standard deviation;\n4. MAE across horizons 7–12;\n5. complexity rank;\n6. candidate ID.\n\nThe resulting winner is:\n\n```text\nseasonal_trend_ols\n```\n\nThe selected model improves pooled MAE by approximately **0.7985 °F**, or **30.3%**, relative to `seasonal_naive_12`.\n\n## Selected forecasting model\n\nThe selected specification is a deterministic seasonal-trend ordinary least-squares model.\n\n| Component | Configuration |\n|---|---|\n| Intercept | Yes |\n| Linear time trend | Yes |\n| Calendar seasonal indicators | February–December |\n| Reference month | January |\n| Seasonal period | 12 |\n| Exogenous predictors | None |\n| Differencing | None |\n| Global learned preprocessing | None |\n\nThe model represents the series as the combination of gradual time movement and a stable recurring monthly pattern.\n\n## Final 1939 evaluation\n\nAfter model selection is complete, the selected specification is fitted once on all 228 development observations and frozen before the final holdout is scored.\n\n| Metric | Backtesting | Final 1939 holdout |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| **MAE ↓** | 1.839438 | **1.526584 °F** |\n| RMSE ↓ | 2.320801 | **1.859967 °F** |\n| Seasonal MASE(12) ↓ | 0.656960 | **0.555495** |\n\nThe final-year metrics are numerically lower than the multi-origin backtesting metrics, but the final holdout contains only 12 observations and should not replace the broader backtesting evidence.\n\n## Final forecast versus observed temperatures\n\n| Period | Observed °F | Forecast °F | Absolute error °F |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1939-01 | 39.4 | 40.3148 | 0.9148 |\n| 1939-02 | 40.9 | 39.7042 | 1.1958 |\n| 1939-03 | 42.4 | 42.7885 | 0.3885 |\n| 1939-04 | 47.8 | 46.8148 | 0.9852 |\n| 1939-05 | 52.4 | 53.1727 | 0.7727 |\n| 1939-06 | 58.0 | 58.6463 | 0.6463 |\n| 1939-07 | 60.7 | 62.5674 | 1.8674 |\n| 1939-08 | 61.8 | 61.0569 | 0.7431 |\n| 1939-09 | 58.2 | 56.9937 | 1.2063 |\n| 1939-10 | 46.7 | 50.2463 | 3.5463 |\n| 1939-11 | 46.6 | 42.9727 | 3.6273 |\n| 1939-12 | 37.8 | 40.2253 | 2.4253 |\n\n![Final 1939 Nottingham forecast versus observed temperature](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-nottingham-monthly-temperatures/main/docs/images/nottem_final_forecast.png)\n\nThe forecast reproduces the broad annual shape, including winter lows, the rise into summer, and the decline toward year end.\n\nThe largest final-holdout absolute errors occur in October and November.\n\n## Understanding the final metrics\n\n### MAE — 1.5266 °F\n\nMAE means the 12 monthly forecasts differ from the observed 1939 temperatures by approximately **1.53 °F on average**, ignoring error direction.\n\nIt is the primary evaluation metric.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n### RMSE — 1.8600 °F\n\nRMSE gives greater weight to larger misses.\n\nIt is higher than MAE because the two metrics aggregate error differently, not because one metric is inherently \"worse.\"\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n### Seasonal MASE(12) — 0.5555\n\nSeasonal MASE scales absolute error by a 12-month seasonal-naive error denominator.\n\nA value below `1.0` indicates forecast error below the seasonal-naive scale represented by that denominator.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n## Backtesting and horizon diagnostics\n\nForecasting performance varies across historical origins and forecast steps.\n\nFor the selected model:\n\n- pooled backtesting MAE is approximately `1.8394 °F`;\n- fold-to-fold MAE standard deviation is approximately `0.3888 °F`;\n- long-horizon MAE across horizons 7–12 is approximately `1.9921 °F`.\n\nHorizon-wise error is not monotonic. A farther forecast step is not automatically more difficult than every earlier step because calendar position and year-specific temperature variation also affect error.\n\nBacktesting diagnostics summarize behavior across multiple historical origins; they should not be confused with the 12 errors from the single final 1939 origin.\n\n## Forecast interpretation\n\nThe frozen model produces an ordered series of 12 future monthly temperature estimates.\n\nFor its read-only inference contract, historical input must contain:\n\n```text\nperiod\ntemperature\n```\n\nwith monthly, unique, strictly increasing, contiguous periods and finite numerical values.\n\nThe forecast horizon is fixed at 12 months.\n\nFor the selected deterministic seasonal-trend model:\n\n```text\nrefit_on_input = false\n```\n\nHistorical values supplied at forecast time establish chronology but do not update the frozen model coefficients.\n\nForecast values should be interpreted as deterministic point estimates under the fitted trend-and-seasonality specification. They are not calibrated uncertainty intervals or guarantees of future temperature.\n\n## What this study demonstrates\n\nThis dataset provides a compact example of disciplined univariate forecasting with:\n\n- a canonical monthly time index;\n- one endogenous numerical target;\n- no source exogenous predictors;\n- strong annual seasonality;\n- explicit temporal rather than shuffled evaluation;\n- expanding-window backtesting;\n- multi-step 12-month forecasts;\n- seasonal-naive and persistence baselines;\n- two AutoReg specifications;\n- three Holt-Winters specifications;\n- two SARIMA specifications;\n- deterministic seasonal-trend regression;\n- explicit documentation of an incomplete candidate;\n- MAE-based practical-tie selection;\n- deterministic tie-break criteria;\n- Seasonal MASE;\n- fold and horizon diagnostics;\n- one-time final-holdout evaluation;\n- ordered forecast-series output.\n\nThe study also demonstrates why time-series evaluation cannot be reduced to a random train/test split or a single final-year metric.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Only 240 monthly observations and 20 annual cycles are available.\n- Development contains 228 observations and the final holdout only 12.\n- The final holdout is sealed for selection from the preparation stage onward but is not exploration-blind because initial retrospective exploration viewed the complete series.\n- The final holdout represents only one calendar year.\n- No exogenous climate, atmospheric, geographic, or measurement variables are available.\n- The selected specification assumes stable recurring monthly seasonality plus linear time movement.\n- One SARIMA candidate is incomplete and ineligible after an optimizer failure blocked continuation.\n- No calibrated forecast intervals or probabilistic uncertainty distribution are produced.\n- Autocorrelation, decomposition, trend evidence, and forecasting accuracy do not identify causal climate mechanisms.\n- Performance under structural climate change, source revision, missing months, measurement changes, or other distribution shifts is untested.\n- No drift-monitoring or scheduled-retraining policy is evaluated.\n- The frozen selected model does not learn from inference-time temperature values.\n- Operational forecasting validity outside this dataset and evaluation design is not established.\n\n## Responsible interpretation\n\nThe evidence supports the following conclusion:\n\n> Nottingham Monthly Temperatures is a small, clean, strongly seasonal monthly series in which annual seasonality dominates the observed systematic structure. Under nine expanding-window backtesting origins, deterministic seasonal-trend OLS achieves a pooled MAE of approximately **1.8394 °F** and improves MAE by approximately **30.3%** relative to the seasonal-naive reference. After the specification is frozen, its single 1939 holdout evaluation yields MAE **1.5266 °F**, RMSE **1.8600 °F**, and Seasonal MASE(12) **0.5555**.\n\nThese results demonstrate effective multi-step forecasting within the documented historical record and evaluation protocol.\n\nThey do not establish causal climate effects, guaranteed future forecast accuracy, calibrated uncertainty, or operational validity under future distribution change.\n"
    }
  },
  "source_draft_schema_version": "1.0.0"
}